What is a Constructor?

    A constructor is a special member of a class that is automatically
    executed whenever an object is created.

    Its primary purpose is to initialize the object's data members.

    Example

        Student s = new Student();

    As soon as the above statement executes,

        Memory is allocated.
        Constructor executes.
        Object gets initialized.
        Reference is returned.

Why do we need Constructors?

    Student s = new Student();

    s.name = "Rahul";
    s.age = 20;

    Easy way to initialize-> Student s = new Student("Rahul",20);



    Everything is initialized immediately.

Advantages

    Initializes object
    Reduces code
    Prevents partially initialized objects
    Makes object creation easier


Rules of Constructor

    Constructor name must be same as class name.
    Constructor has no return type.
    Constructor executes automatically.
    Can be overloaded.
    Cannot be inherited.
    Can call another constructor using this().
    Parent constructor can be called using super().

Types of Constructors

    1. Default Constructor

        A constructor having no parameters.

            Example :

                Student(){

                }

    2. Parameterized Constructor

        Accepts parameters while creating object.

            Example :

                Student(String name,int age){

                }

    3. Constructor Overloading

        Multiple constructors with different parameter lists.

            Example :

                Student()

                Student(String name)

                Student(String name,int age)

        Java decides which constructor to call based on arguments.



What is the difference between a constructor and a method?

        Constructor	                                        Method
    Same name as class	                                Any valid name
    No return type	                                    Has a return type (void or another type)
    Executes automatically during object creation	    Called explicitly
    Initializes object state	                        Performs operations
    Cannot be inherited or overridden	                Can be inherited and overridden (where applicable)


What is constructor chaining?
    Calling one constructor from another using this() (same class) or super() (parent class).

Can constructors be static?
    No. Constructors belong to objects, while static belongs to the class.

Can constructors be overridden?
    No. Constructors are not inherited, so they cannot be overridden.

Why doesn't a constructor have a return type?
    Because Java treats it as a special initialization method. If you add a return type (even void), it becomes a normal method.